WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.420
I want to look really closely

00:00:01.299 --> 00:00:04.360
at that gap between data and

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.100
meaning, because if you pull up

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.080
a table and it shows a

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.319
column labeled temperature, that's

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.059
just a raw number.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.719
Right, it completely lacks the commercial

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.100
context. Exactly. It doesn't tell you

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:17.640
anything about the store experiencing

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.400
that temperature. Well, let's trace

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:20.740
that exact problem because it's a

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.120
great example. If you have a

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.980
spreadsheet and a column simply says

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:26.560
temperature 45, what does your AI

00:00:26.439 --> 00:00:27.100
do with that?

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.160
It just reads the number 45.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.420
Right. And if it's the ambient

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.540
temperature outside a retail location

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.139
on a pleasant spring day, 45

00:00:35.019 --> 00:00:36.960
degrees Fahrenheit is just chilly.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.160
Sure. But if that sensor is

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.400
sitting inside a commercial refrigeration

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.620
unit, holding thousands of dollars

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.339
of premium pharmaceutical supplies

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.600
or, you know, frozen food, then

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.300
45 degrees is a total disaster.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.879
It's a catastrophic failure in progress.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:54.199
But the data itself, the integer

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.639
of 45, is completely agnostic to

00:00:56.519 --> 00:00:57.339
your business reality.
